+# Source-only tiny metric-query proposal
+
+**HOLD: strict collection qualification cannot pass with the pinned collector's internal API-status loss.** No build, syntax check, test, native run, process/device inspection or GPU measurement was performed by this worker. Main owns review and every later execution. This is a source package, not a metric result or permission diagnosis.
+
+`main_controller.py` supplies one bounded, non-root **diagnostic-only** run of the original pinned `sycl_prompt_native16_test --smoke`. It preloads only the native child with the existing `libunitrace_tool.so` SHA256 `8a8fe37441606bf66ab42fc39cc447b5257f48d09f04fbcc60703e30826671eb`. It has no launcher, build, model, retry, privilege or cleanup mode. `passed`, `complete` and `collection_qualified` remain false; successful observable gates produce `diagnostic_complete=true`, status `HOLD_API_STATUS`, exit 2. Other outcomes remain `INCOMPLETE`, including unavailable dependencies, permissions or unsupported operations. No result from this controller unlocks a model metric pass.
+
+`metrics.py` reads the pinned official Linux query format using a complete ordered 83-metric driver catalog, exact PID-named file, finite typed values and exact compute-dispatch counts from `summary.py`. A partial/unknown schema, extra side file, duplicate instance or nonzero sample index fails. PTI quotes names without escaping embedded quotes; the reader splits the known numeric suffix from the right. Detailed compute shapes are removed only for matching the nonverbose summary names. Counter text floats retain PTI's six-decimal limitation.
+
+`summary.py` is a focused adaptation of the pinned project summary/property parser. It retains both timing table, aggregate, numerical and compute-property checks, removes timeline/model processing, and adds bounded decoding. The controller imports only the reviewed project's ownership, identity, map, source, health and fault helpers by exact SHA256. Importing the old controller does not call its main function or import a GPU runtime. Its pinned pure Python timeline module is a transitive import, not an enabled DeviceTimeline collection control.
+
+`enumerate_full.cpp` is an additive revision of `own-metric-enumeration-v1/enumerate.cpp` SHA256 `07c20d237b5e1377c429f5479ee7d029c328a0c422552f248842257ef1afbd9c`. It requests all supported metric properties under the original total 4096 cap and additionally requires FLAT before `zeInit`; it still submits no GPU commands. Its enum-name include is an explicit pinned reference to the unchanged original `result_names.inc`. The original v1 source, executable, receipts and seals were not edited. Main must separately review/build/run a new full-catalog helper under owned control; the v1 controller's hard-coded source path must not be silently reused or its source/pins edited in place. This package does not perform that host build or enumeration.
+
+Before considering even diagnostic execution, Main must:
+
+1. Review `source.patch`, `controller-v3.delta.patch`, `enumeration-v1.delta.patch`, provenance, and the complete source. Pin `SHA256SUMS` externally. No tests were run; Main should syntax-check and exercise malformed/truncated/unknown-column/quoted-kernel/typed-value/coverage cases offline before any device work.
+2. Decide the surfaced API-status issue in `implementation-report.md`. A separate collector/status-observation proposal would need its own explicit review; this package changes neither collector nor provider and has no switch that waives the hold.
+3. If a diagnostic is useful despite the hold, separately close a full-catalog build and enumeration, with the actual build receipt, executable, stdout and normal ownership/fault gates. The build receipt must identify this reviewed package hash, mode `build`, the exact `enumerate_full.cpp` path in its single `host-build` command and `binary_candidate`. The enumeration receipt uses the existing v1 controller record fields, plus FLAT in its actual native environment. This is a receipt contract for Main's separately reviewed controller extension, not an invented result. No automatic transition from build/enumeration to query is provided.
+4. Review all additional metric dependencies without replacing original ELF/file pins. Author an external dependency-review report with `approved=true`, `elf_pins` and `file_pins`; empty mappings are valid only if Main has checked the original inventory suffices. New dependencies require independent provenance/license review. The controller never enrolls live files into the allowlist.
+5. Author a fresh external seal as specified below, select the latest closed sameboot health receipt explicitly, serialize all build/test/GPU/retention work, then launch the controller in an isolated host Python process. Only its native fixture child receives collector controls. There is no execution command with fabricated run IDs, receipt hashes or approval values here.
+
+The external seal must contain `status="MAIN_REVIEWED_SEALED"`, `review_scope="DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY_API_STATUS_HOLD"`, a fresh `run_id` matching `v[1-9][0-9]{0,3}`, current `boot_id`, externally reviewed `reviewed_package_sha256`, UTC `retention_review_utc` within 48 hours, `health_receipt`, `additional_dependency_review` and `catalog`. All artifact pins use the original `{path,realpath,bytes,sha256}` format. `additional_dependency_review` contains `report` (artifact pin) and the exact `elf_pins`/`file_pins` mappings from that report. `catalog` has exactly four artifact pins: `receipt`, `build_receipt`, `binary`, `stdout`. The controller flags are `--seal`, `--seal-sha256`, `--reviewed-package-sha256`, `--run-id`; run it with the reviewed host Python using `-I -B`. The seal belongs outside this source package and cannot rewrite the original inventory.
+
+The run uses native wall and CPU limits of 90 seconds, whole controller limit 300 seconds, decoding at most 15 seconds, 4096 query rows, 64 KiB per decoded line, combined raw output 64 MiB including all collector/partial sides and closure logs, and private files 1 GiB. The raw live work budget is 62 MiB with 2 MiB reserved for closure. It records original oracle stdout, all owned stdout/stderr unchanged, actual mapping snapshots, native argv/PID/start/environment, sameboot journal boundaries/faults, dump checks and owned closure. Unknown mappings fail. Snapshot evidence covers file-backed executable maps observed during the child lifetime, not unloaded libraries, anonymous JIT code or kernel parameter internals. Main retains compact evidence and reviews raw retirement; the code deletes nothing.
